<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>Learning Target 9 Practice</title>

    <p workspace=".2in">
        <em>Identify situations in which it is appropriate to
                            use induction and state the procedure for proving a
                            statement by induction.</em>
    </p>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Need to know</title>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        How to identify when you can prove a statement by induction. You can do 
                        induction when there is a starting place, and an <q>up by one every time</q> or <q>discrete step</q>
                        aspect to the theorem. E.g., if the theorem starts <q> for all <m>n\in\mathbb{N}</m> </q>
                            or <q>for all <m>n\in\mathbb{Z}</m> with <m>n\ge -2</m></q>. You cannot use induction for all
                             <m>n\in\mathbb{R}</m> (no discrete step) nor for all <m>n\in \mathbb{Z}</m> (no starting point).
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                      What the two steps for induction are (base case and inductive hypothesis).
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        How to write out the two steps are, specific to a theorem statement.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        You will NOT need to complete an entire proof by induction.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Practice From Class</title>

        <p>
            Anything in the Chapter 4 worksheets. Weekly Practice 7. See also the extra help for induction under the 
            getting help folder on Blackboard. 
        </p>

    </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>More Practice</title>
    
<exercise>
            <introduction>
                Choose the statements that are appropriate to prove by induction and explain
                why.
                For each statement you choose, state what your steps would be in a proof by
                induction.
                (Your response should be specific to the statement.)
            </introduction>
    
                    <task>
                        <p>
                            For all <m>a\in\mathbb{Z}</m> the equation
                            <m>ax^{3} + ax+a = 0</m> does not have a solution that is a
                            natural number.
                        </p>
                    </task>

                    <task>
                        <p>
                            For each natural number <m>n</m>, <m>3\mid 4^{n} - 1</m>.
                        </p>
                    </task>

                    <task>
                        <p>
                            For all integers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, if <m>x</m> is even and
                            <m>y</m> is odd, <m>x+y</m> is odd.
                        </p>
                    </task>

                    <task>
                        <p>
                            For all natural numbers <m>m</m> and <m>n</m>, if <m>mn</m> is
                            an even integer, then <m>m</m> is even or <m>n</m> is even.
                        </p>
                    </task>

                    <task>
                        <p>
                            For all natural numbers <m>n</m> with <m>n\ge 4</m>,
                            <m>n!&gt;2^{n}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </task>

                    <task>
                        <p>
                            Let <m>a,b\in\mathbb{Z}</m> and <m>n\in\mathbb{N}</m>.
                            For all <m>m\in \mathbb{N}</m>, if <m>a\equiv b \pmod{n}</m>
                            then <m>a^{m} \equiv b^{m} \pmod{n}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </task>

                    <task>
                        <p>
                            For all real numbers <m>x</m>, if <m>x^{2} = 2</m>, then
                            <m>x</m> is irrational. </p>
                        </task>
                    </exercise>

                </paragraphs>
</handout>